  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$7.78B** of paints, pigments, dyes, caulks and inks (HS Chapter 32) in 2025, down **4.1%** on 2024 and 10% below the 2022 peak ($8.64B). Paints and varnishes are the largest part: solvent-based (3208, **$1.64B**), water-based such as latex house paint (3209, $1.09B) and other paints (3210) together make **$2.80B**, 36% of the total. Titanium dioxide and other pigments (3206) follow at $1.48B, down 12.1% as TiO2 pigment (3206.11) fell 18.4% to $873M, then caulks, sealants and fillers (3214, $1.12B), synthetic dyes and organic pigments (3204, $879M) and printing inks (3215, $751M). **Canada ($2.34B) and Mexico ($1.66B) buy 51%** of it; the EU as a whole takes $898M.\n\nTariffs are zero for most of this trade: Canada, Mexico and South Korea charge nothing on qualifying U.S. goods, and the EU cut all of Chapter 32 to zero on July 1, 2026. **China** is the exception: its MFN rate, its 2019 counter-tariffs and a 10% extra duty on all U.S. goods take U.S. solvent paint to 42.5% unless the buyer holds an exclusion, and exports there fell 21.6%. Brazil (up to 14%), India (7.5%–10%) and Japan, Taiwan and the UK (up to 6.5%) charge MFN rates. What usually decides access instead is chemical and product rules — REACH and the paints VOC directive in the EU, VOC limits in Canada, labelling in Mexico — plus U.S. rules such as TSCA section 12(b) export notices and hazmat shipping rules for flammable paint and ink.",
